course: dictionary
lesson: 3360
title: Excited: happy or nervously worked up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Excited is a feeling word with two meanings. Most often it means very happy and full of energy because something good is happening or about to happen, like a birthday or a trip to the zoo.
explain: Excited can also mean nervously worked up. «When you are excited, wait for a pause in the talk before you speak» shows that excited people can be fast and busy inside, so the words around it tell you which meaning fits.
explain: Look at the clues around excited. Good news, a big day or a trip points to happy. A scared animal, a racing heart or a worried face points to nervous. Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Excited means feeling very happy and full of energy because something good is happening or will happen.
fact: Excited can also mean nervously worked up, like a dog that gets excited and barks.
fact: Excited is an adjective (excited, more excited, most excited) and also the past form of the verb excite.
fact: Useful phrases are excited about, excited to, get excited and too excited to sleep.
fact: «Wide, round pupils in good light can mean a cat is scared or excited.» Here excited means worked up, not happy.
exercise: «Slow blinks mean a cat feels safe with you, while wide, round pupils in good light can mean it is scared or excited.» What does excited mean in this sentence? | *Nervously worked up | Bored and sleepy | Very tired after a long walk | Calm and relaxed
exercise: «The red-crowned crane's red patch gets brighter when it is excited.» What does excited mean here? | *Full of strong feeling and energy | Sleepy and slow | Lost and confused | Cold and wet
exercise: «Cockatoos raise their crest feathers when excited or alarmed.» Which word in this sentence is another feeling close to excited? | crest | feathers | *alarmed | Sydney
test: «A spotted hyena's giggle usually means it is nervous, excited or frustrated, not happy.» What do nervous and excited have in common here? | They both mean very hungry | They both mean asleep | They both mean happy and calm | *They both mean worked up inside
test: Which sentence uses excited to mean very happy and full of energy? | The rain made the path wet and cold | *We are excited about the school trip to the zoo tomorrow | The dog was calm and lying in the sun | My pencil is blue
exam: Priya says «When you are excited, wait for a pause in the talk before you speak.» What does excited suggest about the speaker? | They are sleepy and slow to talk | They have no feelings at all | *Their feelings are strong, so they may be talking fast | They are bored with the conversation
